\description{
Fit one linear mixed model per feature of a feature-by-sample table, with a random intercept per group of samples (dataset, donor, day), and test each named coefficient with a Wald t.

mzLib's \code{MixedModel.Fit} models y = X beta + u_group + e for every feature separately: the fixed
effects in your design, plus one baseline per group (a dataset, a donor, an acquisition day)
drawn from N(0, tau^2). Each coefficient is tested by a two-sided Wald t on nlme's "containment"
degrees of freedom. It is \code{nlme::lme(y ~ ..., random = ~ 1 | group)} per feature, which mzLib
reproduces to 1e-5 relative (nlme's own precision), with identical df.
}
\details{
Use it when samples come in groups that share a baseline - runs acquired on the same day,
replicates of one donor, studies in a pooled analysis - and you want each coefficient's effect
*within* groups. \code{\link{stats_fit}} treats every sample as independent, so a day-to-day shift there
either inflates the residual variance or, if the days are unbalanced, leaks into the effect.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_mixed_model_fit}:

  - \code{records}, a data.frame with one row per (reported coefficient, feature) - one block of
    \code{feature_count} rows per coefficient you named, in that order: \code{feature}; \code{coefficient};
    \code{status}; \code{estimate}, the fixed effect within groups in response units per coefficient unit
    (a \strong{log2 fold change} for a 0/1 column on log2 intensities); \code{standard_error} (response
    units per coefficient unit); \code{df} (degrees of freedom), nlme's containment df, a whole
    number, \code{NA} when not fitted or when no positive df exists (a group-level coefficient with
    too few groups); \code{t} (standard errors), the Wald statistic; \code{p_value}, a fraction (0 to 1),
    two-sided on \code{df}, \strong{not adjusted across features}, \code{NA} also when \code{df} is; \code{residual_variance}
    (squared response units), sigma^2 within groups; \code{group_variance} (squared response units),
    tau^2 between the groups' baselines, where 0 is the boundary (no group effect);
    \code{log_likelihood} (log-likelihood), restricted under \code{"reml"}; \code{observed}, samples with a
    finite response; and \code{groups}, distinct groups among them. Statistics are \code{NA} where
    \code{status} is not \code{"fitted"}.
  - \code{responses_file}, \code{design_file} and \code{groups_file}, as absolute paths; \code{grouping}, the groups
    file's label column header; \code{group_names}, in order of first appearance; \code{group_count}
    groups; \code{feature_count} features and \code{sample_count} samples read; \code{sample_names};
    \code{coefficient_names} and \code{tested_coefficients}; \code{estimator}, the estimator mzLib used;
    \code{threads}; \code{missing_count} and \code{zero_count} response cells (cells); \code{status_counts},
    features per status; \code{row_count} rows; \code{column_names}.
  - \code{caveats}: what the model is and is not, plus counts for this input: unfitted features, rows
    with no df, features at the boundary, zero cells.

}
\section{What it is not}{


A random intercept per group and nothing more: no random slopes, nested or crossed groupings, or
residual correlation. Satterthwaite and Kenward-Roger df (lmerTest, pbkrtest) are not
implemented, so p-values differ from lmerTest's. A feature the fixed effects fit exactly is
reported fitted, with a \code{residual_variance} at rounding level and a meaningless \code{t} and
\code{p_value}: check \code{residual_variance} before trusting a very small \code{p_value}.

}
