\section{Parameters: units, ranges and defaults}{
\describe{
\item{\code{responses}}{path; required. stats fit's responses table. A TSV with a header row: the first column is the feature id (unique, any header) and every other column is one sample, named by its header. Cells are used as given, so log-transform intensities first. A blank cell, NA or NaN (any case) is missing and is omitted from that feature's fit, never imputed. A 0 is an OBSERVATION. Blank lines are skipped.}
\item{\code{design}}{path; required. stats fit's design table: a TSV with a header row and one row per sample, the sample name (matching the responses header exactly, rows matched by name) then one column per fixed-effect coefficient, every cell a finite number. Include an intercept column if you want one; do not add a column per group, which the random intercept replaces. mzLib refuses a design that is not of full column rank over all samples.}
\item{\code{groups}}{path; required. A TSV with a header row and one row per sample: the sample name, then EXACTLY ONE column, the sample's group label (a dataset accession, a donor, an acquisition day), whose header names the grouping. The sample names must be exactly the responses' sample columns, in any order. Labels are text, trimmed and compared exactly; NA is a label like any other. An empty label is refused by mzLib (MixedModel.Fit), naming the sample.}
\item{\code{coefficients} (wire \code{--stdin})}{string[]; required; range \code{>= 1 line; each a design column name, none repeated}. The coefficients to report, one design column name per line. The model always fits every design column; the table holds one block of rows per coefficient named here, in this order.}
\item{\code{estimator}}{string; default absent; range \code{reml | ml}. How the variances are estimated: reml is mzLib's VarianceEstimator.Reml (restricted maximum likelihood, not biased downward by the number of coefficients), ml is VarianceEstimator.MaximumLikelihood (biased variances in small samples, but log-likelihoods comparable between designs). Absent, the bridge sends none and MixedModel.Fit's default applies (Reml at d10017ce); the envelope's estimator says which was used.}
\item{\code{threads}}{int; in \strong{threads}; default \code{1}; range \code{>= 1, or -1 for every core}. Features fitted at once. The bridge default is 1 (BULK.md); mzLib's own default is -1. Results are identical at any value (mzLib).}
}
}
