\section{Caveats}{
\itemize{
\item A random intercept per group and nothing more, as nlme::lme(y ~ ..., random = ~ 1 | group): no random slopes, nested or crossed groupings, or residual correlation.
\item Fixed effects are tested by Wald t on nlme's containment degrees of freedom. Satterthwaite and Kenward-Roger (lmerTest, pbkrtest) are not implemented, so p-values differ from lmerTest's. mzLib matches nlme 3.1.169 to 1e-5 relative (nlme's own optimizer precision) in estimates, standard errors, variances, p-values and log-likelihoods, with identical df.
\item group_variance 0 is the boundary estimate. nlme works on the log scale, cannot reach 0, and reports a tiny positive value there instead.
\item Under reml, log_likelihood is the restricted log-likelihood: compare it only between models with the same fixed effects, and use \code{--estimator} ml to compare designs. Under ml, residual_variance is RSS / observed, but standard errors use RSS / (observed - coefficients), as nlme's summary reports ML fits.
\item p_value is not adjusted across features. Send one coefficient's p_value column to stats adjust for Benjamini-Hochberg.
\item A 0 is an observation. Log-transform intensities first, and blank any cell that means 'not measured'.
\item A feature the fixed effects fit exactly (a constant one, say) is reported fitted, with a residual_variance at rounding level (about 1e-16) and a meaningless t and p_value: MixedModel's not_converged guard catches only a residual sum of squares of exactly 0 (d10017ce). Check residual_variance before trusting a very small p_value. Pinned by StatisticsTests.FitMixed_AnExactFit_IsReportedFitted_WithRoundingLevelVariance.
}
}
